## 1. Images

From docker pull (download images) to docker build (create from Dockerfile) and docker tag (versioning), this is where containerization begins. Managing images is the foundation of every Docker workflow.

## 2. Containers

Commands like docker run, docker exec, and docker logs help you create, run, and debug containers. This is where Docker shifts from theory to practice, bringing applications to life.

## 3. Build & Dockerfile

docker build, --target, and --build-arg let you customize how images are built. Mastering this area means cleaner, more efficient Docker images.

## 4. System & Cleanup

Over time, Docker environments get cluttered. Commands like docker system prune and docker df keep your system lean and resource-friendly.

## 5. Networking

From docker network create to docker network inspect, networking is what lets containers talk to each other. Without it, Docker is just isolated boxes.

## 6. Volumes & Storage

Persistent data matters. Commands like docker volume create and docker volume prune ensure you don’t lose critical data (and don’t end up with disk bloat).

## 7. Inspect & Debug

Troubleshooting becomes easier with commands like docker inspect, docker events, and docker diff. They reveal what’s happening under the hood.

## 8. Docker Swarm

For clustering, commands like docker swarm init, docker service ls, and docker stack deploy scale containers across multiple nodes.

## 9. Docker Compose

Finally, the powerhouse for multi-container apps: docker-compose up, docker-compose down, and docker-compose exec simplify orchestration at the project level.

Docker isn’t just a tool, it’s a workflow helper.